y triplets of 8s and 10s will not withstand.\n\nI can make this a four-step sequence.\n1: 888 with 55.\n2: 10-10-10 with 99\n3: KK\n4: 7\nOr, if I want to "bluff", I could mix around the pairs.\nHowever, I have to play one full house with a pair first.\n\nIs there any merit to 7 or 55 first? No, that\'s suicide. If it goes to the opponent, I lose immediately.\nIf I play KK, then they will beat it.\n\nTwo Full Houses are the only choice here, and it makes the best, most structured play.\nIt\'s just about which pair goes with what triplet.\nSince my "7" is out\n\n', 'output': 'Evaluation: My hand is composed of two potential Full Houses (888+Pair, 10-10-10+Pair), a Pair of Kings (KK), and a single 7. I have the initiative (Free Play). Leading with a 5-card combination (Full House) is much stronger than leading a weak single (7) or weak pair (55), as it forces opponents to have a Full House or Bomb to respond. Leading the single 7 would likely surrender control immediately to high cards (A, 2) which I cannot beat.\n\nReason: I should play the lower of my two Full Houses first to clear cards while saving the stronger combination (10-10-10) and my defensive Pair (KK) for later. The combination of 8-8-8 with the pair 5-5 is the most logical opening move. It uses my lowest triplet and lowest pair efficiently.\n\nAnswer: {"cards": ["♠5", "♥5", "♠8", "♥8", "♣8"]}'}
